  if(b.id===SPIRAL_ID)return {...b,name:'Morven Brown · spiral stair / Grad Shop',named:true,aliases:[...(b.aliases||[]),'Grad Shop','C20','Morven Brown spiral staircase'],appearanceReference:SPIRAL_STREET.url,identityReference:GRAD_SHOP_SOURCE,reconstructedMapHoles:SPIRAL_OPEN.map(p=>p.points),heightNote:'The original outer ring, empty hole list, missing level tag and 9.6 m estimated maximum remain. July 2015 public Street View and aerial context guide an open curved concrete stair, blue rails, orange columns, raised annular gallery and glazed northern shop annex. Official university / Arc information identifies the Grad Shop at Morven Brown C20 by the spiral stair. The lower floor anchors to the NSW DEM at the mapped approach endpoint, with a bounded reconstructed floor / approach replacing coarse terrain inside the sheltered passage. The 3.55 m rise, 24 treads, 200 degree turn, radii, gallery levels and unseen elevations are fitted estimates, not surveyed values. Raw DEM samples and mapped path coordinates remain unchanged; current access is not represented.'};
  if(b.id==='osm-way-164558658')b={...b,aliases:[...(b.aliases||[]),'NIDA','NIDA Parade Theatre','National Institute of Dramatic Art']};
  if(NIDA_SCHOOL_IDS.includes(b.id))return {...b,name:b.id===NIDA_SCHOOL_ID?'NIDA · original school':'NIDA · western teaching wing',named:true,aliases:[...(b.aliases||[]),'NIDA','National Institute of Dramatic Art','NIDA school courtyards'],appearanceReference:NIDA_SCHOOL_STREET.url,reconstructedMapHoles:NIDA_SCHOOL_COURTS.filter(p=>p.sourceId===b.id).map(p=>p.points),heightNote:'Original outer perimeter, hole list and two-level map tag remain. The 6.4 m height is the original estimate, not a measured survey. Separate photo-fitted teaching rooms, low hipped roofs / small lanterns, front rooms, open courts and covered galleries replace the generic full-outline extrusion. Hips rise from an estimated 4.7 m eave to a 5.8 m plateau, with lanterns reaching the retained 6.4 m maximum; galleries follow NSW ground at 3.35 m. Public September 2024 Street View, Google aerial roofs, original 1983 parliamentary floor plans and NIDA photographs guide this reconstruction. The 2012 DA’s 14 m James Fairfax Foyer addition is a different component and is not applied here. Exact room uses, courtyard / shade / tree coordinates, unseen elevations and current access remain unverified.'};
  if(b.id===LIBRARY_WALK_ID)return {...b,name:'Library Lawn covered walks',named:true,aliases:[...b.aliases,'Library Lawn covered walkway','Upper campus covered walks'],previousEstimate:b.height,previousVisualEstimate:3.8,height:4.2,heightSource:'estimated',heightQuantity:'Highest walkway cover above local terrain; Library canopy separate',appearanceReference:LIBRARY_WALK_STREET,terrainCanopy:false,modelledCanopyWith:'library',heightNote:'The complete mapped roof-network outline and source footways remain unchanged. The original 9.6 m generic estimate and later 3.8 m flat terrain-following cover remain historical estimates. Corrugated curved roof strips, narrow glazing, two light crossing nodes, paired brown steel supports, lamps and Lawn-side benches are reconstructed from July 2015 Street View and public aerial / university photographs. Walkway roofs rise approximately 3.25–4.2 m above local terrain, including the documented reconstructed Grad Shop lower passage; this is a visual estimate, not a surveyed height. The taller concrete Library entrance canopy uses four mapped roof corners; its detailed roof levels are estimated separately. Detailed roof / structural bays, bench positions and unseen junctions remain approximate. Current access is not represented.'};

  if(b.id===MATHEWS_PAVILIONS_ID){const g=mathewsPavilionsGeometry(b);return {...b,name:'Mathews Pavilions',named:true,campusRef:'E24',aliases:[...b.aliases,'E24','The Pavilions','Mathews Pavilions'],previousEstimate:b.height,previousVisualEstimate:3.8,height:g.maximum,heightSource:'estimated',heightQuantity:'Reconstructed highest roof above DEM foundation',appearanceReference:MATHEWS_PAVILIONS_REFERENCE,heightNote:'The complete mapped roof outline, courtyard hole, missing level tag, roof classification and walkable-under source tag remain unchanged. The original 9.6 m generic estimate and earlier 3.8 m terrain-following cover remain separate historical estimates. A common eave reference and curved roofs replace the flat cover, following labelled public photos, a 29 July 2017 university archive photograph, January 2022 contributor Street View panoramas and Google aerial context. No surveyed numeric roof height was verified: roof reference, pitch / curvature, skylight widths, glazed room bounds, steel branching supports, tree / planter and bench positions are reconstructed. Contributor panorama coordinates and headings are approximate; visible Mathews Tower / Library landmarks and aerial layout guide alignment. Outdoor paving retains the regional DEM. Fine stepped terrace / lower western lightwell geometry remains incomplete; current room access and private interiors are not represented.'};}

  if(b.id===NEW_COLLEGE_ID){const g=newCollegeGeometry(b);return {...b,appearanceReference:NEW_COLLEGE_SOURCE.url,collegeRoomLevels:NEW_COLLEGE_SOURCE.roomLevels,universityGisHeightField:NEW_COLLEGE_GIS.heightField,universityGisFloorCount:NEW_COLLEGE_GIS.floorCount,reconstructedMapHoles:[g.court.points],heightNote:'The 15 m mapped height, five-level OSM tag and complete source envelope remain unchanged. The independent university GIS field gives 16.46 and six floors with unspecified reference / counting conventions. The college describes rooms on levels 1–4, a covered first-floor courtyard and a bridge on level 3. Three brick room rows, a pale green fourth row, two opposed green-glass bays, recessed ground colonnades, white courtyard galleries, translucent raised roof, orange timber bridge, glazed pavilion and striped paving follow June 2023 Street View, official exterior / courtyard photographs and the public college tour. Courtyard / gallery bounds, all detailed floor / roof levels, furniture, vegetation and entrance dimensions are fitted estimates rather than surveyed data. The first-floor court is selectable as a separate map destination; closed entrance doors do not create an invented route through private rooms. Current residence access is not represented.'};}

  if(b.id===SHALOM_ID){const g=shalomGeometry(b);return {...b,aliases:[...b.aliases,'Shalom College','N9'],appearanceReference:SHALOM_COLLEGE.url,photoBalconyRows:3,universityGisHeightField:SHALOM_GIS.heightField,universityGisFloorCount:SHALOM_GIS.floorCount,reconstructedMapHoles:[...SHALOM_GARDEN,...SHALOM_LIGHTWELLS].map(p=>p.points),heightNote:'The source 12 m overall height, four-level OSM tag and complete stepped outline remain. The independent university GIS field gives 19.54 and six floors with unspecified reference / counting conventions; it is retained separately. Official accommodation information identifies balconies on the first and second floors; the northern model has three visible room rows following July 2015 Street View and labelled exterior photographs. Distinct northern balcony wings, central western flat-roof block, lower common-room / entrance wing, two lightwells and a lower Barker Street wing with upswept roofs replace the former uniform extrusion. Internal boundaries, entrance position, roof / floor levels, balcony / rafter spacing, northern garden, street trees, boulders and tree ferns are reconstructed from public photos / aerial context, not surveyed. Purple blossom is a seasonal visual interpretation, not a claim about current vegetation. The February 2022 Barker Street view also identifies the B’nai B’rith Centre frontage. Source footprint and paths stay unchanged; interiors and present residence access are not represented.'};}

